  "notes": "verdict_2026_08_24: EEG 99.7% / 41.3 min. EMG OPEN (453 uV). verdict_2026_08_25: EEG 85.2% / 0.47 h. EMG OPEN (455 uV, 0.0%). verdict_2026_08_26: EEG 92.2%, longest block 91.8 min, 12.26 h inside blocks of 15 min or more -- the second best channel on the rig. EMG still open (458 uV, 0.0%). \ud83d\udd34 THE TETHER DAMAGE IS IN THE DATA, ON THE EMG CONDUCTOR ONLY. C1's EMG sits at 599 uV of 60 Hz against a 5215 uV chip floor with 0.08% railing = the documented damaged/partially-conducting cable signature. Its two channels correlate at |r| 0.091, the working-animal value, NOT the 0.5-0.75 of a shared-path fault. \u26a0\ufe0f C1's EEG RAILED IN 0 OF 7,317 EPOCHS over 20.3 h; max rms 297 uV against a 6377 uV rail. The railing seen on the bench is NOT in this file. Resolve which cage that observation came from before replacing the tether. verdict_2026_09_01: \ud83d\udd27 INTERMITTENT, and the event is in the TETHER BODY. Flat at rms 29 uV for 68 s, then at epoch 17 its EEG (29 -> 189 uV, lf/hf 18.5 vs floor p99 5.90), its EMG (99 -> 567 uV) AND its chip floor (2917 -> 4476 uV) all jump in the SAME epoch. Two roles plus the floor moving together is not an electrode. The identical event appears in the 12:24 file at t = 68 s. Agrees with the 08-27 note that this tether failed structurally at its centre.\n",
